  <h2 id="verdict">The short answer: plan the week, protect two focus blocks a day, say no early and review every Saturday</h2>

  <p>
    Time management is not doing more. It is choosing what matters, protecting time for it and noticing where hours leak. Students and freshers who plan weekly and review honestly usually feel less rushed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The simplest system has three parts: a weekly list of three priorities, two protected focus blocks a day and a short review. Tools matter less than keeping the habit for a month.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Habit</th>
          <th>Our read</th>
          <th>Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Habit"><strong>Weekly plan with three priorities</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Core</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Priorities stop urgent small tasks from eating the week.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Habit"><strong>Two protected focus blocks a day</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Core</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Deep work needs unbroken time.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Habit"><strong>Phone and notification limits</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Core</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Interruptions cost more time than they seem to.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Habit"><strong>Saying no early</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Important</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Late no costs more than early no.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Habit"><strong>Weekly review</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Core</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Review turns experience into better plans.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Habit"><strong>Rest and sleep</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Core</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Tired work takes longer.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="evidence">How to check where your time goes, without quoting statistics</h2>

  <p>
    For one week, write down what you do in blocks of thirty minutes. At the end of the week, mark each block as useful, needed or wasted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most people find two or three leaks, such as unplanned phone time or switching between tasks. Fix the biggest leak first.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page uses no survey figures. Your own week is the evidence.
  </p>
  <p class="note-line">
    This page does not quote statistics. The method is the evidence: your own week of time notes.
  </p>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A perfect schedule fails. A simple one you keep beats a clever one you drop.
    </p>
  </div>

