  <h2 id="sales">Who rewards good time habits, and how they notice</h2>

  <p>
    Teachers, employers and clients notice people who deliver on time and say early when something slips. They judge you by reliability, not by busyness.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic habit is a Sunday plan: three priorities, two focus blocks a day and one thing you will not do.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and time management</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools can plan your week and draft messages. This raises the value of choosing priorities and keeping the habit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Prefer tools that reduce friction, such as reminders and summaries. A staged path helps: now, use AI for plans and notes; later, handle the trade-offs that need your judgment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Common mix-ups to avoid</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Planning every minute</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Plans break on the first surprise.</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Plan blocks and leave slack.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Starting with a new app</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">The app becomes another task.</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Use paper for a month first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Skipping sleep to gain hours</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Tired work takes longer.</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Protect sleep as a priority.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to practise free, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Skill is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free paper notebook or a free calendar app.</li>
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM lessons on study skills and management.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube lessons on planning.</li>
    <li>A study partner for weekly review.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In four weeks, plan each Sunday, keep two focus blocks a day and review every Saturday, then note what changed.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="week">A sample week you can copy and change</h2>

  <p>
    A plan is a set of blocks, not a timetable of every minute. Edit this sample to fit your classes or job.
  </p>
